{"type": "final", "turn": 6, "content": "\n\nHere's the comparison of your two 2025 PA Karst predictions (both completed, same period Jan\u2013Dec 2025):\n\n**What was done:** fetched both results, then ran a cross-model pair comparison on a 6\u00d76 grid over their shared extent (~10,091 km\u00b2; 25 cells compared, 11 dropped as no-data).\n\n| Metric | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Property A (KarstBinary) | `sample_karst_score`, regression 0\u20131 |\n| Property B (KarstNumber) | `sample_number`, regression 0.2\u20131.2 |\n| Correlation (A vs B) | -0.0172 |\n| Agreement within tolerance 0.1 | 0.0 |\n| Cells compared / dropped | 25 / 11 |\n\n**Where they differ:** effectively everywhere \u2014 they share almost no signal. The correlation is -0.0172 (essentially zero), so the two maps do not rise and fall together at all. Note that the two models predict *different quantities* (`sample_karst_score` vs `sample_number`), so the mean difference, RMSE (0.618) and per-cell gaps between them are not meaningful gaps between the same thing \u2014 only the near-zero correlation is a fair reading.\n\n**Which is right:** I can't tell you, and no tool can without ground-truth labels. This is model-vs-model agreement, not accuracy, and neither result has a reference to check against. If you want a real answer, I can plan a targeted labeling design (`olmoearth_plan_label_sample`) for one or both maps \u2014 e.g. pick the most suspect windows from each \u2014 and after you label N of them I can compute an honest error rate per map. Want me to set that up?", "forced_by_turn_cap": false, "grounding_revised": false, "seq": 14, "t": 136.302, "seconds": 9.451}
